WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:07.380
THE MONSTER:&nbsp;

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:26.640
Johnson &amp; Johnson is likely the most recognizable&nbsp;
household brand in the entire world. They’ve been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:32.100
around since 1886 and they dominate dozens of&nbsp;
industries from pharmaceuticals and medical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:38.100
devices to consumer health and baby shampoo.&nbsp;
They’re currently the 9th largest company in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:46.200
the world coming in at a monstrous marketcap&nbsp;
of $432 billion. But, it’s not just their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:53.040
marketcap that’s monstrous because really, the&nbsp;
entire company is monstrous. While their fluffy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:59.280
white baby powder and no tears shampoo come of as&nbsp;
seemingly harmless, if you look at their history,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:06.000
you’ll quickly find that the company is anything&nbsp;
but that. Like any corporate giant, J&amp;J has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:10.920
been willing to pull whatever strings and go to&nbsp;
whatever lengths to maximize their marketshare and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:16.530
profits. Whenever someone like Apple or Microsoft&nbsp;
does this, while it’s frowned upon by consumers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.530 --> 00:01:22.320
it’s usually not that big of deal. Worst case&nbsp;
scenario, you end up with an iPhone that you can’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:28.200
repair or software that’s filled with bloatware.&nbsp;
But, when a healthcare company like J&amp;J does this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:36.000
the consequences are much more deadly literally.&nbsp;
Over the years, J&amp;J has been allegedly responsible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:42.120
for the death and suffering of millions of&nbsp;
people. And to make things worse, these disastrous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:49.020
consequences were often not a result of an honest&nbsp;
mistake or oversight. More times than not, they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:56.160
were a result of negligence and ignorance. This is&nbsp;
why many argue that J&amp;J has the bloodiest hands in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.160 --> 00:02:03.020
the fortune 500. But, how did “a family company”&nbsp;
evolve into being such a ruthless predator?

00:02:03.020 --> 00:02:05.400
WHOLESOME BEGINNINGS:&nbsp;

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:13.440
Taking a look back, Johnson &amp; Johnson did start&nbsp;
off as a wholesome family business. As the name&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:18.600
suggests, it was founded by the Johnson family,&nbsp;
but there were actually three Johnsons in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:24.540
business, not just two. This included the brothers&nbsp;
Robert Wood Johnson I, James Wood Johnson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:30.660
and Edward Mead Johnson. While J&amp;J did start of&nbsp;
a family business, it never really went through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.660 --> 00:02:35.940
that stage of being a bootstrapped garage&nbsp;
startup. You see, Robert was already a pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:40.740
successful businessman having founded a company&nbsp;
called Seabury &amp; Johnson while produced sterile&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.740 --> 00:02:47.760
surgery equipment. By 1878, this company alone&nbsp;
was producing $10,000/month in profits. That&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:54.420
itself is a solid number, but when you account&nbsp;
for inflation, it becomes $296,000 per month. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.420 --> 00:03:00.360
yeah, Robert was killing it. Unfortunately though,&nbsp;
the founders would end up having a disagreement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:06.600
and Robert would eventually leave the business,&nbsp;
but not before becoming extremely wealthy. Combine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:11.640
this with the fact that his brothers were well&nbsp;
off and talented in their own right and starting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:17.280
up Johnson &amp; Johnson was a breeze. The trio had&nbsp;
capital, knowledge, experience, and connections&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:22.860
from day 1, so they hit the ground running.&nbsp;
They hired 14 employees and launched a line of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:29.040
ready to use surgical supplies and that marked the&nbsp;
beginning of J&amp;J in 1886. Over the next few years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:33.660
the brothers aggressively grew their product line&nbsp;
and made history within the surgical industry.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.660 --> 00:03:38.880
They produced the world’s first sterile surgical&nbsp;
products including sterile sutures, cotton, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:46.080
gauze. And in 1894, they launched their signature&nbsp;
baby powder. By this point, J&amp;J had only been in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:52.560
business for a mere 8 years, but they were already&nbsp;
employing 400 people and running 14 buildings. Not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:57.480
to mention, they were already becoming a household&nbsp;
name. But, just because they were successful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.020 --> 00:04:04.380
didn’t mean that they were evil or unethical.&nbsp;
In fact, there’s very little evidence suggesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.380 --> 00:04:09.420
that any of the brothers had ill intentions or&nbsp;
immoral motivations. There’s actually a bunch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.420 --> 00:04:13.980
of evidence suggesting the exact opposite.&nbsp;
During the Spanish-American war for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:20.400
J&amp;J donated 300,000 surgical dressings and created&nbsp;
trauma stretchers for the soldiers. They also made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:24.720
substantial donations to the relief efforts&nbsp;
of the 1900 Galveston Hurricane and the 1906&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:30.540
San Francisco Earthquake. And these weren’t just&nbsp;
for publicity either, and you could tell based on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:36.180
how they treated their employees. For example,&nbsp;
when the 1901 smallpox epidemic hit, J&amp;J would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.180 --> 00:04:42.420
sponsor the vaccination of all their employees.&nbsp;
Similarly, during the great depression, J&amp;J didn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.420 --> 00:04:49.200
even lay off a single employee. They actually&nbsp;
raised wages by 5%. J&amp;J was also ahead of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:54.180
time when it came to incorporating women into the&nbsp;
workforce. 8 out of their first 14 employees were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.180 --> 00:04:59.760
women. And over the decades, women accounted for&nbsp;
roughly half of the company’s workforce and led&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:05.220
a quarter of their departments. But then, how&nbsp;
did such an innovative, forward thinking, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.220 --> 00:05:11.340
generous company devolve into what they are today?&nbsp;
Well, it all started with the brothers dying off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.340 --> 00:05:17.940
one by one. Robert was the first to go in 1910,&nbsp;
dying of chronic kidney disease. 22 years later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.940 --> 00:05:22.920
James would actually die while returning from&nbsp;
a vacation on the RMS Majestic. And finally, in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.920 --> 00:05:28.980
1934, Edward would pass away from a heart attack.&nbsp;
Within just 10 years of the last founder dying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.980 --> 00:05:34.920
J&amp;J would be taken public in 1944, and that&nbsp;
marked the beginning of their moral freefall.

00:05:34.920 --> 00:05:36.220
SLOW DETERIORATION:&nbsp;

00:05:39.180 --> 00:05:45.180
Johnson &amp; Johnson didn’t just become immoral&nbsp;
overnight though. After all, the company was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.180 --> 00:05:50.040
led by the brothers’ children who carried many of&nbsp;
the same values and principles as the brothers’&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.040 --> 00:05:55.980
themselves. In fact, when the company was taken&nbsp;
public, Robert’s son, Robert Wood Johnson II,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.980 --> 00:06:01.680
wrote an ethical guidelines book called “Our&nbsp;
Credo”. This document outlined how choices had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:06.300
been made at the company for the past 60 years,&nbsp;
and how they should continue being made moving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.300 --> 00:06:11.160
forward. Essentially, the Credo advises leaders&nbsp;
at the company to put the needs and well-being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.160 --> 00:06:16.500
of the people they serve over profits. And it&nbsp;
looks like this is what they were doing at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.500 --> 00:06:23.100
beginning. A perfect example of this was the&nbsp;
Chicago Tylenol deaths. In 1982, 7 indiviuals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.100 --> 00:06:29.580
in the Chicago metropolitan area would all die&nbsp;
shortly after taking Extra Strength Tylenol. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.580 --> 00:06:34.020
turns out that someone had taken the Tylenol&nbsp;
off store shelves, laced them with cyanide,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.020 --> 00:06:40.440
and then put them back up. After hearing about&nbsp;
this, J&amp;J didn’t even hesitate to recall all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.440 --> 00:06:45.420
31 million bottles of Tylenol on store shelves&nbsp;
and replace them with new Tylenol that came in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.420 --> 00:06:50.760
tamper proof packaging. This immediate action&nbsp;
was praised around the world as being the gold&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.760 --> 00:06:57.180
standard for corporate crisis management. But,&nbsp;
with time, it seems that J&amp;J has drifted further&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.180 --> 00:07:01.980
and further away from this gold standard&nbsp;
starting off with small infractions here and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.980 --> 00:07:08.100
there. A perfect example of this is the illegal&nbsp;
marketing of Risperdal. If you’re not familiar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.100 --> 00:07:13.380
with Risperdal, it’s an antipsychotic that’s&nbsp;
used to treat schizophrenia, bipolar disorder,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.380 --> 00:07:19.140
and irritability caused by autism. Risperdal has&nbsp;
been instrumental for many people over the years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.140 --> 00:07:25.200
but like any medication, it comes with side&nbsp;
effects and is not applicable to everyone.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.200 --> 00:07:31.380
One group of people that should not take Risperdal&nbsp;
is older adults with dementia related psychosis.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.380 --> 00:07:37.980
And this was crystal clear since the medication&nbsp;
was approved by the FDA in 1994. Nonetheless,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.980 --> 00:07:42.540
J&amp;J paid Omnicare who is the largest&nbsp;
pharmaceutical supplier for nursing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.540 --> 00:07:49.140
homes to promote Risperdal. And it wasn’t till&nbsp;
a whistleblower outed J&amp;J in 2010 that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.140 --> 00:07:56.340
got caught. And the penalty for this despicable&nbsp;
behavior was a mere $2.2 billion. For perspective,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.340 --> 00:08:01.680
J&amp;J profits $20 billion per year which means that&nbsp;
this penalty cost them a little more than a month&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.680 --> 00:08:07.860
of their current profits. This is just the tip&nbsp;
of the iceberg though. J&amp;J has also been linked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.860 --> 00:08:13.020
with foreign bribery. Apparently, they’re&nbsp;
sales reps paid doctors in Greece, Poland,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.020 --> 00:08:18.420
and Romania to sell medication and medical&nbsp;
devices. They also bribed officials in Iraq to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.420 --> 00:08:26.280
win contracts. The fine for all of this? A measly&nbsp;
$78 million. Aside from this, J&amp;J has also gotten&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.280 --> 00:08:31.020
a lot more aggressive when it comes protecting&nbsp;
their patents. When we’re talking about patents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.020 --> 00:08:36.060
relating to medical devices and medications,&nbsp;
that’s atleast understandable. They usually have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.060 --> 00:08:41.700
to invest hundreds of millions if not billions&nbsp;
to create these products, so it makes sense that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.700 --> 00:08:46.440
they don’t want to get ripped off right after&nbsp;
launch. Something that doesn't make sense though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.440 --> 00:08:52.020
is that fact that J&amp;J has previously tried to&nbsp;
prevent the American Red Cross from using the Red&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.020 --> 00:08:59.100
Cross. If you didn’t know, J&amp;J actually owns the&nbsp;
trademark for the Red Cross symbol, and in 2007,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.100 --> 00:09:05.580
they sued the American Red Cross to stop using the&nbsp;
emblem. Yeah, I don’t think anything more has to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.580 --> 00:09:11.280
be said about that. While these petty lawsuits,&nbsp;
corruption, and marketing are inexcusable in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.280 --> 00:09:16.920
themselves, it turns out that J&amp;J actually&nbsp;
had much darker secrets behind the curtains.

00:09:16.920 --> 00:09:20.880
BECOMING THE PREDATOR:&nbsp;

00:09:20.880 --> 00:09:27.060
One of the biggest lawsuits that has plagued&nbsp;
J&amp;J recently is the baby powder lawsuit. It is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.060 --> 00:09:32.520
alleged that the talc based powder that is iconic&nbsp;
to their baby powder is actually contaminated with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.520 --> 00:09:38.580
asbestos. The obvious reasoning for this is that&nbsp;
the mines in which talc is mined are often lined&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.580 --> 00:09:45.780
with asbestos. So, it’s only natural that the talc&nbsp;
is also contaminated with asbestos. But, it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.780 --> 00:09:50.700
assumed that J&amp;J had procedures and protocols&nbsp;
in place to ensure that asbestos wouldn’t show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.700 --> 00:09:57.180
up in the final product. J&amp;J of course insists&nbsp;
that they do have such proecdures in place and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.180 --> 00:10:06.240
they deny all allegations. But, this didn’t stop&nbsp;
40,000 cancer patients from banding together and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.240 --> 00:10:13.140
filing lawsuits against J&amp;J. And the evidence that&nbsp;
has surfaced since then is disturbing to say the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.140 --> 00:10:21.180
least. Allegedly, J&amp;J found asbestos in their talc&nbsp;
multiple times as early as 1972, but they didn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.180 --> 00:10:27.120
even notify the FDA. Instead of making change&nbsp;
though, J&amp;J has opted to use legal loopholes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.120 --> 00:10:32.040
to avoid liability which came to light just&nbsp;
earlier this year through Reuters. Apparently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.040 --> 00:10:38.160
J&amp;J created a highly confidential plan called&nbsp;
“Project Plato”. The plan entailed creating a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.160 --> 00:10:43.380
new subsidiary and then shifting all the lawsuits&nbsp;
to this new subsidiary. From there, the subsidiary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.380 --> 00:10:47.400
would declare bankruptcy which would push the&nbsp;
cases to bankruptcy court and substantially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.400 --> 00:10:53.280
reduce payouts. There are even allegations that&nbsp;
J&amp;J tried to get a federal judge to block Reuters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.280 --> 00:10:59.820
from publishing their findings. I don’t know about&nbsp;
you, but that’s highly sus to say the least and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.820 --> 00:11:06.120
that’s not the only scandal that J&amp;J has been&nbsp;
caught up in either. J&amp;J has also been embroiled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.120 --> 00:11:11.640
in opioid lawsuits for the past several years&nbsp;
as well. If you’re not familiar with opioids,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.640 --> 00:11:17.220
its a substance that acts on opioid receptors to&nbsp;
produce morphine-like effects. It’s usually used&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.220 --> 00:11:23.100
in pain relief medications and anesthesia and&nbsp;
it can be instrumental when used properly. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.100 --> 00:11:27.660
if it ends up in the wrong hands, it can&nbsp;
quickly lead to drug addictions and deaths due&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.660 --> 00:11:32.820
to overdose. You would think that pharmaceutical&nbsp;
companies would be extremely careful when it came&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.820 --> 00:11:40.260
to distributing opioids, but they were actually&nbsp;
doing the exact opposite. Between 2006 and 2015,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.260 --> 00:11:45.900
opioid companies spent $880 million on&nbsp;
lobbying efforts to keep opioids easily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.900 --> 00:11:50.700
accessible. Fortunately, all of this did&nbsp;
eventually come to light and pharmaceutical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.700 --> 00:11:56.160
companies including J&amp;J agreed to a settlement&nbsp;
of $26 billion at the beginning of this year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.160 --> 00:12:05.040
but this doesn’t really mean anything because the&nbsp;
damage has already been done. Since 1999, 760,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.640 --> 00:12:12.780
people have died due to drug overdose and ⅔ of&nbsp;
these deaths in 2018 involved opioids. Personally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.780 --> 00:12:17.400
I don’t think J&amp;J can ever wipe&nbsp;
their hands clean after this one.

00:12:18.660 --> 00:12:23.700
